WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.789
A repeatable intelligence analysis routine begins with the decision.

00:00:03.789 --> 00:00:07.889
Before collecting more information, define the intelligence question, audience, timeframe,

00:00:07.889 --> 00:00:09.939
and level of detail needed.

00:00:09.939 --> 00:00:15.678
Ask what decision the analysis should support and what would make the answer useful.

00:00:15.678 --> 00:00:21.417
This keeps the work focused and prevents collection from becoming a substitute for reasoning.

00:00:21.417 --> 00:00:23.467
Gather only authorized, relevant information.

00:00:23.467 --> 00:00:27.156
Use lawful, ethical, policy-approved sources such as public reports,

00:00:27.156 --> 00:00:31.256
internal authorized telemetry summaries, incident notes, vendor advisories, risk registers,

00:00:31.256 --> 00:00:33.715
business context, and sanitized case data.

00:00:33.715 --> 00:00:36.995
Protect sensitive information and minimize what you collect.

00:00:36.995 --> 00:00:42.324
Analysis quality does not require invading privacy or gathering information outside approved boundaries.

00:00:42.324 --> 00:00:43.964
Evaluate sources and information.

00:00:43.964 --> 00:00:46.423
Separate source reliability from information credibility.

00:00:46.423 --> 00:00:51.343
Ask whether the source has relevant access, whether the claim is plausible,

00:00:51.343 --> 00:00:56.262
whether it is timely, whether it is corroborated, and what limitations apply.

00:00:56.262 --> 00:01:01.181
Document those limits so others can review and update the assessment later.

00:01:01.181 --> 00:01:02.001
Organize evidence.

00:01:02.001 --> 00:01:05.691
Build a timeline, source table, evidence matrix, issue tracker,

00:01:05.691 --> 00:01:08.560
or structured note set that preserves provenance.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:11.840
Good organization reveals sequence, gaps, patterns, and contradictions.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.759
It also helps the team work under pressure without losing track of

00:01:16.759 --> 00:01:19.629
what is known and what is assumed.

00:01:19.629 --> 00:01:22.498
Separate facts, assumptions, hypotheses, judgments, and recommendations.

00:01:22.498 --> 00:01:23.318
Label assumptions.

00:01:23.318 --> 00:01:24.958
Test hypotheses against evidence.

00:01:24.958 --> 00:01:29.877
Use structured analytic techniques when the problem is complex, uncertain, or important.

00:01:29.877 --> 00:01:33.977
Consider alternatives and ask what evidence would change the assessment.

00:01:33.977 --> 00:01:40.126
This reduces the chance that the first plausible answer becomes the final answer too quickly.

00:01:40.126 --> 00:01:40.946
Communicate clearly.

00:01:40.946 --> 00:01:43.815
Lead with key judgments or bottom-line findings.

00:01:43.815 --> 00:01:47.505
Explain evidence, assumptions, confidence, uncertainty, implications, and next steps.

00:01:47.505 --> 00:01:49.965
Match the product to the audience.

00:01:49.965 --> 00:01:53.244
Avoid unsupported claims, vague wording, and hidden uncertainty.

00:01:53.244 --> 00:01:58.163
A good intelligence product makes reasoning visible enough that decision makers can

00:01:58.163 --> 00:02:00.213
act and reviewers can understand.

00:02:00.213 --> 00:02:01.443
Finally, review outcomes.

00:02:01.443 --> 00:02:06.772
After the decision, ask whether the analysis helped, what changed, what assumptions held,

00:02:06.772 --> 00:02:11.282
what evidence was missing, and what should be improved next time.

00:02:11.282 --> 00:02:15.381
Intelligence analysis is disciplined reasoning in service of better decisions.

00:02:15.381 --> 00:02:18.251
Its value comes from evidence, transparency, humility,

00:02:18.251 --> 00:02:21.940
and a constant effort to make judgment more useful.
